I have been playing around with various character types in Slash'em on
the forkexec server, and decided to try my luck at something hard; a
vampire. But with something easier; a wizard. The big lifesaver of the
early game was starting with +Create Monster (make food fast!) and
+Resist Poison (to be able to eat anything with blood in it). I prayed
occasionally for food, but Create Monster made the whole thing much
more relaxing.

The second goodie was bones in the Mall (thanks Tret), with a bag of
holding, a luckstone, speedboots and a cornuthaum. My two starting
u?oRemove Curse made them all quite useable at once. The bag contained
several more spellbooks, and the bookstore in the Mall had even more.
Suddenly I had an excellent selection including mmissile, endure cold,
detect monster and others. The magic lamp I kept for lighting for the
time being... That was quite a find, come to think of it... The
bookstore also had a pretty expensive spellbook that I didn't buy until
about the time of the quests, which turned out to be +Identify.

I found a healthstone early in the mines which I bagged for the time
being, but my first prayer at the coaligned altar in minetown decursed
also that. The gods obviously don't mind drained corpses, so I stayed
at that altar until I had Magicbane. Izchack had some magic candles,
but I avoided using the lamp for a wish for the time being - there
wasn't really anything that I was desperate for..

Sokoban yielded a second bag of holding, and with the Slash'em sokoban
spoilers back online, I didn't need to abuse my luck here. Yes. I'm
spoiled. But there were definitely suprises along the way...

I cleared out the end of the mines - the winecellar, which meant some
early holy water as well, and Ruggo's gnomes and gnoll went down to
magic missile.

At this point I decided I needed reflection, so I rubbed the blessed
magic lamp - and got a djinni pet. *sigh* Ow well, the bad luck didn't
last:

The ratlevel was my own bones of an archeologist; "oReflection and
GDSM, as well as a touchstone. Mirrorbright was stashed in my bag as
useless for a spellcaster, but the Gauntlets of Defence were donned
with joy. Probably more stuff, but nothing worth mentioning.

Level 16 had a coaligned temple. Yay! I set up stash there for most of
the midgame, sacrificing until I got Deluder, which I used for the rest
of the game. A few prayers later I got crowned. At this stage, hunger
was no longer a problem though, and I'd gotten my first messages about
overfeeding during attacks when clearing out Grund's Stronghold. (Those
bars were a pain. Utterly. Some giants helped me find the doors by
crashing through them, luckily. My shadowwolf? of Anhur also got itself
killed in the battle.) The priest was given all my money as I gathered
it up.

Ludios was around level 13 I think, and the money from also that went
to the priest.

I had gotten the Quest message on level 18 and on level 17 suddenly
stumbled into a magic portal. I found myself face to face with various
undead, and suddenly this Vecna fellow attacks me with pools of water
and other stuff. I _knew_ about the aligned quests - I just thought
they'd give off a warning or a call or something like the role quest.
My first suprise. That was actually fun! Vecna ended up on the wrong
side of a boulder in a passageway, and I magic missiled him to
destruction, while hovering over Elbereth. Key of Chaos and the Hand,
as well as XL 13.

Then I decided that this was hard on the Power, so I went to complete
the role quest. Once I had the Eye, I went searching for the other two
aligned quests. The Beholder was pathetic - or maybe I was overprepared
with a highly enchanted unihorn at this stage..

Nightmare's level was the biggest fun nethack has given me in ages.
Corridors that disappeared, food and coins that suddenly attacked, a
cursed -4 wormtooth named Storm Brand, empty wands of nothing, probing
and opening, a co-aligned priest that suddenly turned hostile.
Absolutely full of suprises! Nightmare hit hard though, and I might not
have survived that battle without the Gauntlets providing half-damage.

All quests completed, I continued exploring. I went down every
sidebranch; wyrm-caves, spider-caves, and the lost tomb. Plenty of
dragon lairs, but I had my GDSM, and didn't need the scales. The Finger
took out most of what I encountered, with a little help from other
spells against those that resisted. Slash'em has loads of spells -
knowing what to use is almost impossible, so I just stuck to a few
favourites in the end.

The black market had loads of interesting stuff, and I wasn't about to
pay for it, so I id'ed all books and rings for +passwall or
=oPolyControl. I found the latter, so I gathered everything I wanted
(including Hellfire and Deathsword) and polymorphed into a xorn. Oh
darn, there goes the shirt and armour. Hadn't thought of that after
playing too many doppelgangers (who remember to undress)...

Oh well. I went to the closest cleared dragonlair and picked up silver
scales. I took everything back to stash and made SDSM, and swapped the
"oReflection for the Eye of Aethiopica.

A throwaway comment in an earlier YAAP had alerted me to the real prize
of the Sunless sea, so I cast magic mapping (which for some strange
reason also works as a +detect treasure) and found the position of the
magic lamp. But I was naturally flying so what now? I tried freezing
the surrounding area and making a pit. And came up against the Bug
probably everyone here knows about. I poly'ed into a xorn - and still
couldn't reach the bottom. I poly'ed back and defroze the water, but
the pit wouldn't fill. Finally I zapped it with a /oTeleport and hoped.
It got beached, and the lamp was mine. Still didn't use it though.

The temple of Moloch was ridden of its unholy creatures and undesirable
priesthood, and I decided that with the branch-teleport property of the
Eye, this would be the ideal stash for me. I lit the level, converted
the altar, and dragged one of the chests out of the storage room. The
bunch of books fleshed out my repetoire, but the robes of power had too
poor AC and too little effect on my failure-rates to be worth it. My
stash was moved down here, except what I knew I'd be using on the
ascension run (4!obooze + 4?oGoldDetection, plus the useless yet
scorepadding artifacts). I picked up a couple of new pets, courtesy of
Anhur.

Medusa and the Castle were pitiful, though I ran as much as I fought.
By this time I was getting a little tired of just hitting stuff, and
just wanted to be finished. The wand was at (0:3) and I used 3 wishes
on markers to write more spellbooks, as well as one on a helm of
brilliance. The cornuthaum is stylish at +6, but less usefull for a
highlevel wizard who can cast magic mapping with the flick of a finger.

The demons started off with Orcus, but never really got dangerous. Not
even Demogorgon who kept attacking my displaced image, and went down to
the back-bounce of my third Cone of Cold spell. Well out of range for
his own attacks. Deluder is a good artifact, and Demogorgon needs to
use his eyes better. :) Cure Sickness is a must have though. The
unihorn doesn't always work, and non-spellcasters may have more trouble
than I did...

Vlad's throne provided me with a wish which I used on a figurine of a
Solar. Solaris ascended with me, though she dumped her starting sword
for a trident for some reason. She also wore a [oReflection, and was
given +2 leather gloves from me. She wouldn't take any other armour.
Excellent pet, though she offed one of Anhur's minions as her first
task in my service. Perhaps a bit too dedicated..?

Frankensteins tower was interesting, but the reflective golems were
quite a handful for me. I ended up teleporting them away, or leaving
the job to Solaris.

Dispater was wacked with my off-hand (and Solaris) while I was busy
talking to a gypsy. I tried wishing for the Wallet of Perseus, but far
too many artifacts had already been generated for that to stand a
chance.

I did a last cleanup of the loot in my stash before going for the VS
and the Wizard. At around this time I alchemized more gain level and
finally got the body-spells to skilled. Passwall suddenly became my
personal favourite. Even the undiggable walls in the demon-lairs were
no hindrance! I suspect this spell alone cut down the ascensionrun with
many many turns.

I first found the Square, then fingered Rodney. The Book was soon mine,
and I and Solaris made our way to the Sanctum. Passwall helped me get
around doors and most of the crowd, as well as a dash of Charm Monster.
I fingered the Priest, and picked up the amulet. The way back up was
uneventful, though Rodney doubletroubled at my stash on level 16. I let
Solaris kill off the shopkeepers in the Mall, and I took the Sunsword
which had been lying in a shop there.

I had too much with me and was burdened through the planes. I spent
quite a few wand-charges, just to get stuff out of my way, but, hey!
that's what I brought those wands for! Fire was the easiest with the
portal 10 steps away. Intrinsic flying is nice.

Astral was worse. I went left first, Famine and a Solar together. Then
a basilisk gave me the slowing messages. Since I'd never before been in
this situation with a vampire, I'd brought lizard corpses, but the
blood of course had coagulated. I had no !oAcid with me. I decided my
only chance was prayer, which worked. Then I killed the basilisk first.
The Solar creates a pool below me, but since I flew, I wasn't worried.
Then I make the second big mistake. I forget about the pool, decide I
need to become unburdened - and drop my blessed bag of holding splash!
into the pool. Damned. I zapped it with a /oTeleport, with no idea
where it would end up. Anyway, I managed to crawl to the altar, after
teleporting away most monsters, low on mana and almost no wand-charges
left. It was Neutral.

I cast detect treasure, and discovered my bag was near the center
altar. Well, I'd be going there next, so I'd pick it up if I survived
that far. At this point I only had 4!oFull Healing in my oilskin bag,
but was wearing an amulet of life saving just in case... Thank Anhur I
didn't need it. (It's one conduct I've kept in all ascensions, as far
as I can remember - never having my life saved.)

I spent some time being ineffectually clawed by a shimmering dragon
standing in the outermost centre door, while building up more power,
then I went in, teleporting away everything I could. I picked up my bag
here. The altar was Chaotic, so I whistled in Solaris (the other Angel
hadn't survived), and sacrificed the Amulet.




